{"title":"2004 IMO Shortlist Problems/N2","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(<i>Russia</i>)\nThe function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/d/c/cdc865749d6ff9f09734c920de909b8617aeb94d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle \\psi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"12\" height=\"16\" /> from the set <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/9/639a63f4b76f5cd6b54773491e1074fa937e2eaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"16\" height=\"13\" /> of positive integers to itself is defined by the equality\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/7/3/773d75691c4871b54501175b9ef58c60d5760ab0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi(n) = \\sum_{k=1}^{n}(k,n), \\qquad n \\in \\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"221\" height=\"48\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/a/9/ca9c72330807add54d5b8c684d22a7ee4bf3281e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle (k,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"18\" /> denotes the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Greatest_common_divisor\" title=\"Greatest common divisor\">greatest common divisor</a> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/6/de61dde472a066a5b90a2ff0be7a2f756110011b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle k$\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.\n</p><p>a) Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/c/a4cc6fb25dcb28783e828e2f1d55dc6abfa824e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle \\psi(mn) = \\psi(m)\\psi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"160\" height=\"20\" /> for every two relatively prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/b/a6ba6e10f75c645d94284601671b46fd6db04332.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n \\in \\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" />. <br />\nb) Prove that for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/7/e075dccc91d8f4e4fb475269236481a1e321227e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\in \\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" /> the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/e/9beed4af1bf32c7e6d148be420bf6dcf3ddf5583.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle \\psi(x) = ax$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" /> has a solution. <br />\nc) Find all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/0/7/e075dccc91d8f4e4fb475269236481a1e321227e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a \\in \\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" /> such that the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/e/9beed4af1bf32c7e6d148be420bf6dcf3ddf5583.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle \\psi(x) = ax$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" /> has a unique solution.\n</p><p><i>This was also </i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> be a divisor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" />.  We note that for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/7/dc7f5e7dbd17728c1bce03bd9a021f4d35a637ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\leq n/d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/8/0a8d60ee74576a011c8ffe725d3b5063d7cb3b14.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(dk,n) = d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"86\" height=\"18\" /> if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c325612684d41304b9751c175df7bcc0f61f64f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"14\" /> is relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/e/d6e1dd5b15cd4e83b38cb96a1add8c1c0b395b54.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n/d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"29\" height=\"18\" />.  It follows that each divisor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"13\" height=\"10\" /> is found in the sum <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/b/c/7bc543250b1e9c2a8da3b26779c31758af6a87b9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sum_{i=1}^{n}(k,n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"67\" height=\"48\" /> exactly <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/2/bd23833320a68e54a26fcd9c03e9da2d3d22bb66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(n/d )$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"53\" height=\"18\" /> times, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/8/f28e35d575996547cf296769de076c7ae35d2bff.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(m)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" /> is defined as the number of natural numbers less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> and relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> (the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Euler_Totient_Function\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Euler Totient Function\">Euler Totient Function</a>).  It follows that\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/c/07c9044a6647e000cf339bb5e3d37dba99e0012e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi (n) = \\sum_{k=1}^{n}(k,n) = \\sum_{d \\mid n} d \\phi(n/d)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -24px\" width=\"246\" height=\"52\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>In other words, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/d/8ada738001410f131563551fb68731e4f302048d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"12\" height=\"16\" /> is the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Dirichlet_convolution\" title=\"Dirichlet convolution\"> convolution</a> of the identity function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/6/3664684c081bba983e12e26d9a90811fe4f6e247.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f: n \\mapsto n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"16\" /> and the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/1/2c175f60eecef1de7560c3bdea495d69f26f719d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"13\" height=\"18\" /> function.  Since these are both <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Multiplicative_function\" title=\"Multiplicative function\">multiplicative functions</a>, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/d/8ada738001410f131563551fb68731e4f302048d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"12\" height=\"16\" /> is also multiplicative, which is part (a) of the problem.  For the next parts, we note that the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/2/d02ce40a428851f63f6375d0e5888af4c46009bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi(x) = ax$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" /> is equivalent to the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/5/45538e20f2389f6c1486b012b521e780e622eeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\psi(x)}{x} = a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"75\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>\nWe now note that for a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and a non-negative integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/a/a3a59bb1293ee3f6dec19de4019a7178874219ae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />, </p><center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/5/2f55b1b08b1f744ea051a0ed065108ae1ec80a44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi(p^k) = \\sum_{i=0}^{e}p^i \\cdot \\phi(p^{e-i}) = p^e + \\sum_{i=0}^{e-1}p^i(p^{e-i} - p^{e-i-1}) = p^e + e(p^e - p^{e-1})$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"558\" height=\"50\" />. </center>\n<p>Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/d/8ada738001410f131563551fb68731e4f302048d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"12\" height=\"16\" /> is multiplicative, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/8/e1872fffb1f668107d44c2561dfceb7f43f28416.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\prod_{i=1}^k p_i^{e_i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"45\" height=\"51\" /> is the prime factorization of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />, then\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/7/2475413f6246e420925bd128294ff2982cb6e7d2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi(x) = \\prod_{i=1}^k [(e_i+1)p_i^{e_i} - e_i\\cdot p_i^{e_i}]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"244\" height=\"51\" />,\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>and\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/d/f7dae2de6fb447cb0c46ac47114fd7eb95092d00.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\psi(x)}{x} = \\frac{\\prod [(e_i+1)p_i^{e_i} - e_i \\cdot p_i^{e_i}]}{\\prod p_i^{e_i}} = \\prod \\left(e_i \\frac{p_i-1}{p_i} + 1 \\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"415\" height=\"43\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>From this we can see that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/0/a/f0a25406d97153c5c9051a6f8598e669b7235156.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 2^{2(a-1)}$\" width=\"83\" height=\"16\" /> is always a solution to the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/5/45538e20f2389f6c1486b012b521e780e622eeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\psi(x)}{x} = a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"75\" height=\"38\" />, which solves part (b).  Let this solution to the equation be the <i>canonical solution</i>.  However, we also note that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/4/2/242655c42a1ec7f4ae9f35d4f57445f496ed7359.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2r +1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"48\" height=\"13\" /> is an odd postive integer, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/2/f/12f49b114c6bd80c4cfc59b0a1ff277d4e6e8987.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 3^{3r}$\" width=\"56\" height=\"15\" /> is another solution to the equation.  In particular, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> has an odd prime factor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/b/37b452baa1091e074e629116ee52ab780c7e339a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2r+1 &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"81\" height=\"13\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/d/9bdfe8f10aaeb376715f460d4dd69493cd88fe5f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = 2^{2[a/(2r+1) -1]} \\cdot 3^{3r}$\" width=\"164\" height=\"16\" /> is a solution distinct from the canonical solution.  Thus if the only solution to the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/5/45538e20f2389f6c1486b012b521e780e622eeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\psi(x)}{x} = a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"75\" height=\"38\" /> is canonical, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> cannot have any odd divisors greater than 1, i.e., <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> must be a power of 2.\n</p><p>We now claim that for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/8/9b87dbc03936b137eee58bb5056e15179e30fbc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 2^k$\" width=\"50\" height=\"15\" />, the equation has no non-canonical solutions.\n</p><p><b>Lemma.</b>  Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/e/e7e48449a24d0fb260da65d6fddf13fb03f84470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p_1, \\ldots, p_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"74\" height=\"11\" /> be odd primes, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/9/8298c0ab96fa1090f51bc5371fca5cdafd70524c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_1, \\ldots, e_k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"72\" height=\"11\" /> be positive integers.  If\n</p>\n<center> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/c/0ccb94e95b396a8a880e50cdb21b40e2a7a8862a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\prod_{i=1}^{k}\\left(e_i \\frac{p-1}{p} +1\\right) = \\frac{p}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -20px\" width=\"177\" height=\"51\" /> </center>\n<p>and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/0/6f06a183d1092b27328c12a97322763ac8db97a2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p, q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"26\" height=\"11\" /> are relatively prime positive integers, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is divisible by an odd prime.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i>  We first note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e86667e2b1a073a57c6a9e702e2e205a028acb36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e_i \\frac{p_i-1}{p_i} + 1 \\ge \\frac{p_i-1}{p_i} + 1 &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -16px\" width=\"233\" height=\"40\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/b/02b15d1a41ce816d04457697670229efde467df0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{q} = \\prod \\left( e_i \\frac{p_i-1}{p_i} +1 \\right) &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"217\" height=\"43\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/a/97aed5fce925fca0a180dd911b435c3416a3cbce.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p&gt;q\\ge 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"75\" height=\"15\" />.  Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> must be divisible by some prime.  But since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/9/b/89b27130bc19ff8dce3d0b336689ee6b29b2e170.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{p}{q} = \\prod \\left( e_i \\frac{p_i-1}{p_i} + 1 \\right) =  \\frac{ \\prod [e_i(p_i-1) + p_i] }{\\prod p_i}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"349\" height=\"43\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> divides a product of odd numbers, so it cannot be divisible by 2.  Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is divisible by an odd prime.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p>Now, suppose that for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/b/8/9b87dbc03936b137eee58bb5056e15179e30fbc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 2^k$\" width=\"50\" height=\"15\" /> there exists some non-canonical solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/a/ccada11db7b2b90693e2fac4f887a57fce6f96bf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"16\" height=\"10\" /> to the equation.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/e/4be3576755fa27bda24eed22ca3dec1c4172ec27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi(2^j) = \\frac{j}{2} + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"110\" height=\"37\" />, the only value of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> which is a power of 2 and is a solution is the canonical solution. Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/5/3/553720d2a738c12faa1b5a1ea3e4cca212712470.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x_1 = 2^c \\cdot n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"81\" height=\"15\" />, for some nonnegative integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" /> and some odd integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/2/65262bf247c4a3c6114a8223d249dc9702619d3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" />.  But by our lemma, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/3/f331396a7d83262328124145748bf40e6a23fd68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\psi(2^c\\cdot n)}{2^c\\cdot n} = \\left(\\frac{c+2}{2} \\right) \\cdot \\frac{p}{q}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"188\" height=\"43\" />, for some relatively prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/6/1/0615acc3725de21025457e7d6f7694dab8e2f758.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$q$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> is odd.  But since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/a/76a90a15c99f15e989c8de477647c56b7b075ad7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\psi(x_1)}{x_1}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"46\" height=\"41\" /> is an integer and there are no factors in the denominator common to the odd prime which divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/6/f/36f73fc1312ee0349b3f3a0f3bd9eb5504339011.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/f/d/efdae183526f33ca03798d0456d3590838a740dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{\\psi(x_1)}{x_1} = 2^k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"88\" height=\"41\" /> must be divisible by an odd prime, a contradiction.  Thus the canonical solution to the equation <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/0/2/d02ce40a428851f63f6375d0e5888af4c46009bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\psi(x) = ax$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" /> is unique if and only if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is a power of 2.  Q.E.D.\n</p><p><br />\n<i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N2&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N2&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems\" title=\"2004 IMO Shortlist Problems\">2004 IMO Shortlist Problems</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=191396#p191396\">Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?t=16599\">Further Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017071212\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: []\nCPU time usage: 0.036 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.063 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 417/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 373/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 14755/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   22.305      1 -total\n 79.88%   17.817      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n 10.44%    2.328      1 Template:Halmos\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:4996:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017071212 and revisio